#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129976} :activity/id #uuid "9eb9ab54-1ec4-5368-a2e1-150d6811c312" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048584 :topic/id #uuid "abbdc596-7744-5a68-9999-a74874659d19" :topic/math-academy-id 2018} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130353 :tutorial/content "Rotation-scaling matrices are involved in an important theorem regarding complex eigenvalues.\n\nTo sketch out the key idea, let's consider the matrix \n\n![](images/06/06a28b9b9d8db65a4214ed1d446c68f288978acc59afd3720873da7f825efe13.svg)\n\nwhich has the complex eigenvalue \n\n$$\n\\lambda=1-2\\textrm{i}={\\color{blue}1}+({\\color{red}-2})\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding complex eigenvector \n\n![](images/1b/1b7aa45404b3e6e02cb9802da371f6c74f6848f4b5601261f2257e12b7d362d0.svg)\n\nNow, we construct a rotation-scaling matrix $C$ using the real and imaginary parts of the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ as follows:\n![](images/0f/0f29362b05dd18b6a8a307f531cea6d59c083301703285bde41a9488f228c386.svg)\nWe also construct a matrix $P$ whose columns are the real and imaginary parts of the corresponding eigenvector:\n![](images/d1/d1b2efc5b22e34c0bb4cb93a4777c8d8139ca0047253b7177bd1809a272f5b37.svg)\n\nThe **rotation-scaling theorem** states that $A$ can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\nA=PCP^{-1}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's check that this is true in this case:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PC{P}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d79a993f-cb2c-5ec9-a24b-ddc7bd94485d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3594 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "19750f8a-a131-5f4c-9488-2c2c664f3b38" :step/math-academy-id 20167 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129978}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129977 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129134 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128945 :question/id #uuid "1b13d9bb-9bcf-50be-a58d-39e82d99b9bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5530"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562518}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "e7048fe4-4391-5227-903b-216c1855d7e0" :step/math-academy-id 20168 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130354 :tutorial/content "Let's now formally state the rotation-scaling theorem:\n\n*Let $A$ be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix with real entries. If $\\lambda$ is a complex (not real) eigenvalue of $A,$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda,$ then *\n\n$$\nA = PCP^{-1}\n$$\n\n*where*\n![](images/74/74b63ce33cfa7779a6668a22974b709d549843a221c3b13067887e8193c6e6f0.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When two matrices $A$ and $C$ are related via the equation $A = PCP^{-1},$ we say that they are **similar.**\n\n- The rotation-scaling theorem tells us that every $2 \\!\\times\\! 2$ real matrix with complex (non-real) eigenvalues is similar to a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- We call the right-hand side of $A = PCP^{-1}$ the **rotation-scaling form** of $A.$ We can also refer to this as the **rotation-scaling decomposition** of $A.$\n\n- The matrix $P$ *must* be constructed from the eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue defined by $C.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "09a26114-9068-50b4-897e-76504d4f7e68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3595 :tutorial/title "The Rotation-Scaling Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "6e5f25d2-d5e2-5abe-a1c6-08572dd50606" :step/math-academy-id 20169 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129979}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129135 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128946 :question/id #uuid "26e641c9-bda5-5b93-8137-f4ec744ddb5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5531"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dcdd3f4-c03e-5887-a493-05598dbe4fc3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049192}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562511}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Rotation-Scaling Form"} :step/id #uuid "b2e60b35-aad7-5404-bbeb-360484d2e855" :step/math-academy-id 20170 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129977}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129980 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129136 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128947 :question/id #uuid "75f39ec3-0963-5555-b418-d5b2df3be0ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-5532"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048973}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562450}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Only One Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "380e2b24-5a1b-580a-9d3e-20419aa321c4" :step/math-academy-id 20171 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129981}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129981 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130355 :tutorial/content "Suppose $A$ is a real $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrix. To interpret the rotation-scaling form $A=PCP^{-1},$ we should bear in mind the following:\n\n- The matrix $C$ rotates vectors $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^2$ and then scales them.\n\n- We can think of $P$ as representing the following change of variable:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = P\\mathbf u \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad  \\mathbf u = P^{-1}\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ can be thought of as a change of variable from $\\mathbf x$ to $\\mathbf u,$ followed by a rotation and scaling before returning to the original variable.\n\n![](images/ab/ab40869137296cea63fe209a842a4713fcae4fbf53c5089e6722b70bdf3f3230.png)\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/74/74c17c58d6b39ff6a13ec56e135ec17ce0f260d2e9d90e562544637f6e677db0.svg)\n\nThis is a real matrix with complex eigenvalues and is thus similar to a rotation-scaling matrix. \n\nUsing the techniques described in this lesson, we can show that the rotation-scaling decomposition of $A$ is\n\n![](images/15/1542ac2d3f79e1494ecf9e67dda3219966efdf76267ebda82d7689555dc4df30.svg)\nThe matrix $C$ can be written explicitly as a constant multiple of a rotation matrix as follows:\n![](images/f0/f0d562472e2b230a0cd393829ab4a5dec380e6fd1e2a797af4f1f651aae88f75.svg)\n\nThus, the matrix $C$ represents the following:\n\n- a rotation of $\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac\\pi 6$ counterclockwise about $O,$ and\n\n- an enlargement of scale factor ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\nThe image of a unit square centered at the origin under the action of $A$ can therefore be decomposed as follows:\n\n![](images/92/92f2ab84b9ac8f36a93c42bf93b70b08a3e64eb3920a8bd2d5276217d936ab79.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5e77afe5-c30a-56bd-95a6-037f1a903eda" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3596 :tutorial/title "The Geometric Interpretation of Rotation-Scaling Form"} :step/id #uuid "ed8aecde-9d1f-5061-afa2-aa5cc7c1036e" :step/math-academy-id 20172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129214 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129033 :question/id #uuid "e0469305-b1d9-5430-9911-2f0c209b3264" :question/math-academy-id "e-8734"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048305}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410856}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"} :step/id #uuid "7ce1fcbb-0bb6-5b74-ba7c-4006f8e5f481" :step/math-academy-id 32487 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129980}}] :activity/title "Reducing Real 2x2 Matrices to Rotation-Scaling Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129361}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410860} :activity/id #uuid "5f5420bc-3099-5b53-8e6a-d43b1a5bffb0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048584 :topic/id #uuid "abbdc596-7744-5a68-9999-a74874659d19" :topic/math-academy-id 2018} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a822e0b-1fa5-5287-afb1-796b6d1e0af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410833} {:answer/id #uuid "39b2aad9-d90d-5e0c-99c0-086cce1f5858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410838} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f2b609-e44a-59ef-8d01-1bf7e729ec81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410843} {:answer/id #uuid "b6dd3430-18a3-5d4c-aa84-56246e5e6e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410844} {:answer/id #uuid "d396ac8a-9e62-5ef7-aa22-08d3e2840f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410846}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410844} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba30525c-f325-567a-9297-5b7c22b96813" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b89eb3d0-aaac-5e32-8106-610f887f220d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99416" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=-1-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\text{i} \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "43b5ac79-b939-5882-be87-3661f9387201"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410859 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3060d69a-8868-5172-98ad-30a1f7c52b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410837} {:answer/id #uuid "4fac5dc4-ba16-5d80-bb5f-c21a117180c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410839} {:answer/id #uuid "5205fd9c-21af-5e9d-977c-cdc6794be310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410841} {:answer/id #uuid "95fee729-2135-5651-b276-0d9f45ed5e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410842} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e1a656-0f4d-5746-87e0-6a88b18aafd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410845}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410837} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cbd9e54-5f08-5851-844d-53c1919540be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "59a82201-03c5-57db-ad1f-7c04fbb98da0" :question/math-academy-id "q-39157" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -10 \\\\ 17 & -10\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda =3-\\text{i},$ and that $A=PC{P}^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3-\\text{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=3-\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -10 \\\\ 17 & -10\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}13+\\text{i} & -10 \\\\ 17 & -13+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(13+\\text{i}){x}_{1}-10{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 17{x}_{1}+(-13+\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{10}(13+\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}(13+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=10$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 13+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{10}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{{13}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{10}{17}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8a7b4be3-057a-5a01-ae4d-c3c2e06c7bcc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410861}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410860 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129666 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ce9065-737f-5539-9ccc-e2b738124790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410831} {:answer/id #uuid "1b240860-8981-5813-8afb-56eb81d9d5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410835} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ea0660-35e1-50a9-b795-bedb0a6fec90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410847} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8d4b33-9126-5017-a544-498f72884d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410848} {:answer/id #uuid "ed49cec1-2e8a-5d58-9643-ab871cbaf80d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410849}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410848} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca3e15c9-c3c6-56d5-be44-2ff142682b75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dce754c8-5be0-5c82-8349-b2f897b46d75" :question/math-academy-id "q-38571" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -6 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7-6\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -6 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "aae2eb64-abfd-507c-b3bc-9c0cd712cf93" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410859}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410861 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096e7527-4c01-5dce-8ff8-e0db3c75370d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410832} {:answer/id #uuid "0a8fe61d-12cc-5e71-bf5f-8ade179fac6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410834} {:answer/id #uuid "1c87451a-2d1c-5373-8af0-e4594174b876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410836} {:answer/id #uuid "5113c383-2506-5331-9225-ebb1f5af24a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410840} {:answer/id #uuid "f16edcb8-7daa-5bb4-b079-ab44c7accef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410850}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410850} :answer-field/id #uuid "8400b796-2f04-536c-986d-13f15ae5b122" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d6880ebc-9daa-5cd0-b5d7-2100fa039d06" :question/math-academy-id "q-99345" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c04d526e-a848-55ef-9d6a-e6653cc7bbac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410858}}] :activity/title "Reducing Real 2x2 Matrices to Rotation-Scaling Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410830}]}
